 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	/**
     2	 * The single door to the Telegram Bot API for anything carrying a reader-visible body.
     3	 *
     4	 * WHY THIS EXISTS, in the order the reasons were learned:
     5	 *
     6	 * Review passes 29 through 35 each found the same shape by a different route. A guard was placed on
     7	 * one send path and the path was called guarded; a second egress existed (pass 29); the guard ran
     8	 * before a formatter that changed the representation (pass 33); the lint that policed the guard could
     9	 * not prove its own claims about call resolution or method classification (passes 34, 35). Six
    10	 * patterns, one class: the guarantee was distributed across senders, so proving it meant proving a
    11	 * property of every sender, and every proof was weaker than it read.
    12	 *
    13	 * A boundary does not have that problem. If exactly one function may reach the network, then "is the
    14	 * payload checked" stops being a question about six call sites and becomes a question about one.
    15	 *
    16	 * WHAT IT CHECKS, and why here rather than earlier. It reads the request as Telegram will read it —
    17	 * the query string and the serialised body, the exact bytes about to go on the wire. Every earlier
    18	 * placement checked a representation that something later could still change: the caller's text, then
    19	 * the formatter's output, then the object handed to fetch. This is the last one.
    20	 *
    21	 * WHAT PASS 36 FOUND, because moving a boundary is not the same as closing a class:
    22	 *
    23	 *   1. The door checked ONLY a non-empty string body. Telegram accepts parameters in the URL query,
    24	 *      as form encoding, and as multipart — so a reader-visible method sent any of those other ways
    25	 *      reached the network unchecked. The door was one encoding wide.
    26	 *   2. Method and host recognition were case-SENSITIVE while Telegram's dispatch is not. `sendmessage`
    27	 *      is dispatched by Telegram and missed the field map, which returns silently on an unknown key.
    28	 *   3. Moving the boundary DELETED the closed-world method check the previous per-sender lint
    29	 *      performed. A newly-used reader-visible method passed through the approved door, received no
    30	 *      decision, and left while the lint stayed clean — a guard that nothing guards, which is the
    31	 *      exact defect this file's own header claimed to have eliminated.
    32	 *
    33	 * All three share one root: the door treated what it could not check as fine. The rule below is the
    34	 * opposite, and it is the only rule here that matters — AN UNCHECKABLE REQUEST IS A CLOSED DOOR.
    35	 *
    36	 * WHAT THIS DOOR DOES NOT COVER, decided rather than missed (review pass 38 finding 4). A non-Bot URL
    37	 * that REDIRECTS into a Bot API method is classified once, on the initial URL, and `fetch` follows the
    38	 * redirect without a second decision. Manual redirect handling would close it and would also break the
    39	 * file-download callers that legitimately redirect.
    40	 *
    41	 * It is left open because it is outside this guard's purpose: the guarantee is that THIS AGENT does not
    42	 * send a message a reader receives as nothing. A redirect crossing into the Bot API requires an actor
    43	 * who already controls a response this agent fetches — at which point the invisible-payload guard is not
    44	 * the control that matters. Stated so the next reader can disagree with the judgment rather than
    45	 * discover the gap.
    46	 */
    47	import {
    48	  assertOutgoingPayloadVisible,
    49	  emitInvisiblePayloadRefusal,
    50	  READER_VISIBLE_TELEGRAM_PARAMS,
    51	  readerVisibleFieldsFor,
    52	  NO_READER_VISIBLE_FIELD_TELEGRAM_METHODS,
    53	} from './invisible-payload.js';
    54	
    55	/**
    56	 * Any Bot API URL — `api.telegram.org/bot<token>/<method>`. Case-INSENSITIVE: a hostname is
    57	 * case-insensitive by RFC and Telegram accepts method names in any case. A matcher stricter than the
    58	 * dispatcher it models produces requests Telegram honours and this file never sees.
    59	 */
    60	const BOT_API_HOST = 'api.telegram.org';
    61	/**
    62	 * STATED OPEN — a self-hosted Bot API server is invisible to this door.
    63	 *
    64	 * Telegram supports replacing the cloud endpoint with a local server, and both this recogniser and
    65	 * `scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs` hard-code the host above. An agent configured against a
    66	 * local server would have EVERY send classified as non-Telegram and pass unchecked, and the lint would
    67	 * print clean over it.
    68	 *
    69	 * Confirmed open by review passes 47 and 48. Recorded here rather than only in the review archive
    70	 * because a reader arriving at this constant is the person who needs to know it, and an open item that
    71	 * lives only in an archive is one nobody meets. Closing it means taking the host from configuration,
    72	 * in BOTH the runtime and the lint, so the two cannot drift apart.
    73	 */
    74	/** Lowercased method → canonical spelling, so lookup matches Telegram's case-insensitive dispatch. */
    75	const CANONICAL_METHOD: ReadonlyMap<string, string> = new Map(
    76	  [...Object.keys(READER_VISIBLE_TELEGRAM_PARAMS), ...NO_READER_VISIBLE_FIELD_TELEGRAM_METHODS]
    77	    .map((m) => [m.toLowerCase(), m] as const),
    78	);
    79	
    80	/**
    81	 * Recover the API method from the URL rather than trusting a caller-supplied label.
    82	 *
    83	 * A caller passing the wrong method name would select the wrong reader-visible field, and the check
    84	 * would silently examine nothing. The URL is what Telegram dispatches on, so it is the only
    85	 * description of the request that cannot disagree with the request.
    86	 *
    87	 * Returns the CANONICAL spelling when the method is known, so a case variant cannot slip past a
    88	 * case-sensitive field map; returns the raw spelling when unknown, which `telegramFetch` refuses.
    89	 */
    90	export function methodFromTelegramUrl(url: string): string | null {
    91	  // Parse rather than pattern-match the raw text. Review pass 37 finding 3: a regex anchored to the
    92	  // literal host missed spellings `fetch` normalises before dispatch — an explicit `:443`, leading
    93	  // whitespace, a percent-encoded or upper-case host. Telegram received those requests; the door
    94	  // returned null and skipped every check. Whatever `fetch` will send is what must be inspected, so
    95	  // the parser `fetch` uses is the one that decides.
    96	  let parsed: URL;
    97	  try {
    98	    parsed = new URL(url.trim());
    99	  } catch {
   100	    // @silent-fallback-ok: a string `URL` cannot parse is not a Bot API URL, and `fetch` will reject
   101	    // it too — so classifying it as "not ours" is the accurate answer, not a swallowed failure. The
   102	    // request never reaches Telegram either way; there is no degraded delivery to report.
   103	    return null;
   104	  }
   105	  if (parsed.protocol !== 'https:' && parsed.protocol !== 'http:') return null;
   106	  // A terminal dot is the DNS root and denotes the SAME host; `new URL()` preserves it while an exact
   107	  // string compare rejects it, so the request reached Telegram and the door returned null (pass 39 F2).
   108	  const host = parsed.hostname.toLowerCase().replace(/\.$/, '');
   109	  if (host !== BOT_API_HOST) return null;
   110	  let pathname = parsed.pathname;
   111	  // @silent-fallback-ok: a malformed percent-escape is judged in its RAW form rather than dropped.
   112	  // Refusing here would skip every check on a request Telegram still dispatches, which is the exact
   113	  // bypass shape this door exists to close — so the fallback is toward MORE checking, not less.
   114	  try { pathname = decodeURIComponent(pathname); } catch { /* judge the raw form */ }
   115	
   116	  // Model Telegram's extraction rather than pattern-matching a shape: it strips an optional `test`
   117	  // segment and treats the ENTIRE remaining path as the method name (pass 40 F1/F4). The previous regex
   118	  // consumed only an alphabetic PREFIX, and its optional group backtracked so that `/bot<token>/test/`
   119	  // resolved to the method `test` — which then refused as unclassified, hiding the root case entirely.
   120	  const afterToken = /^\/bot[^/]+\/(.*)$/.exec(pathname);
   121	  if (!afterToken) return null;
   122	  let rest = afterToken[1].replace(/^test(?:\/|$)/, '').replace(/\/+$/, '');
   123	  if (rest === '') return null;
   124	  return CANONICAL_METHOD.get(rest.toLowerCase()) ?? rest;
   125	}
   126	
   127	/** A Bot API URL whose path carries a token but NO method segment — the shape Telegram resolves from a
   128	 *  `method` argument instead. */
   129	export function isBotApiRoot(url: string): boolean {
   130	  let parsed: URL;
   131	  try { parsed = new URL(url.trim()); } catch {
   132	    // @silent-fallback-ok: same reasoning as the classifier above — an unparseable string is not a Bot
   133	    // API root, and a degradation report about a request that never happens is noise, not signal.
   134	    return false;
   135	  }
   136	  if (parsed.hostname.toLowerCase().replace(/\.$/, '') !== BOT_API_HOST) return false;
   137	  let pathname = parsed.pathname;
   138	  try { pathname = decodeURIComponent(pathname); } catch {
   139	    // @silent-fallback-ok: judged in its RAW form rather than dropped, exactly as in the classifier —
   140	    // the fallback is toward MORE checking, since refusing here would skip every check on a request
   141	    // Telegram still dispatches.
   142	  }
   143	  // The test-environment form `/bot<token>/test/` is ALSO a root: Telegram strips the `test` segment
   144	  // and then resolves the method from a parameter exactly as it does for production (pass 40 F1 — the
   145	  // tests covered test-paths-with-a-method and production-roots-with-a-parameter, never the
   146	  // intersection, which is where the bypass lived).
   147	  return /^\/bot[^/]+\/(?:test\/?)?$/.test(pathname);
   148	}
   149	
   150	export class TelegramEgressError extends Error {
   151	  constructor(message: string) {
   152	    super(message);
   153	    this.name = 'TelegramEgressError';
   154	  }
   155	}
   156	
   157	/**
   158	 * Name a duplicated TOP-LEVEL key in a JSON object body, or null. Deliberately a scanner rather than a
   159	 * parser: it only needs to answer "is the effective value ambiguous here", and a wrong answer in the
   160	 * ambiguous direction costs a refusal rather than a delivery.
   161	 */
   162	function duplicateTopLevelJsonKey(body: string): string | null {
   163	  const seen = new Set<string>();
   164	  let depth = 0;
   165	  let inString = false;
   166	  let escaped = false;
   167	  let key: string | null = null;
   168	  let buf = '';
   169	  for (let i = 0; i < body.length; i += 1) {
   170	    const c = body[i];
   171	    if (inString) {
   172	      // Compare DECODED keys: `text` and `\u0074ext` are the SAME key to `JSON.parse` and to Telegram,
   173	      // and the first version appended raw source characters, so an escaped spelling read as distinct
   174	      // and the duplicate went undetected (pass 39 F3).
   175	      if (escaped) {
   176	        escaped = false;
   177	        if (c === 'u') { buf += JSON.parse(`"\\u${body.slice(i + 1, i + 5)}"`); i += 4; continue; }
   178	        buf += ({ n: '\n', t: '\t', r: '\r', b: '\b', f: '\f' } as Record<string, string>)[c] ?? c;
   179	        continue;
   180	      }
   181	      if (c === '\\') { escaped = true; continue; }
   182	      if (c === '"') { inString = false; key = buf; buf = ''; continue; }
   183	      buf += c;
   184	      continue;
   185	    }
   186	    if (c === '"') { inString = true; buf = ''; continue; }
   187	    if (c === '{' || c === '[') { depth += 1; continue; }
   188	    if (c === '}' || c === ']') { depth -= 1; continue; }
   189	    if (c === ':' && depth === 1 && key !== null) {
   190	      if (seen.has(key)) return key;
   191	      seen.add(key);
   192	      key = null;
   193	    }
   194	  }
   195	  return null;
   196	}
   197	
   198	/** Parameters Telegram will read, gathered from BOTH places it accepts them. */
   199	function collectParams(url: string, body: RequestInit['body']): {
   200	  params: Record<string, unknown>;
   201	  uncheckable: string | null;
   202	} {
   203	  const params: Record<string, unknown> = {};
   204	
   205	  // The query string is a first-class way to pass Bot API parameters. The first version of this door
   206	  // ignored it completely, so `sendMessage?text=<invisible>` was never examined.
   207	  //
   208	  // Two corrections from review pass 37. The FRAGMENT never goes on the wire — `fetch` strips it — so
   209	  // reading it as payload let visible fragment text mask an invisible query value (finding 2). And
   210	  // Telegram appends URL arguments BEFORE body arguments while its accessor returns the FIRST match,
   211	  // so on a conflict the QUERY value is the one that gets sent; the first version let the body
   212	  // overwrite it and inspected a value Telegram would never use (finding 1). Query is therefore
   213	  // collected LAST here, with `queryKeys` marking what must not be overwritten.
   214	  let search = '';
   215	  try {
   216	    search = new URL(url.trim()).search;
   217	  } catch {
   218	    const q = url.indexOf('?');
   219	    const h = url.indexOf('#');
   220	    if (q >= 0) search = h > q ? url.slice(q, h) : url.slice(q);
   221	  }
   222	  const queryParams = new URLSearchParams(search);
   223	
   224	  /**
   225	   * Overlay the query LAST: on a conflicting key, the value Telegram sends is the query's.
   226	   *
   227	   * And take the FIRST occurrence of a repeated key, not the last. Telegram's accessor returns the
   228	   * first match; iterating `URLSearchParams` yields every occurrence, so assigning in loop order left
   229	   * the LAST one in the object. Measured before this fix, both directions were wrong:
   230	   * `?text=<invisible>&text=visible` was SENT while Telegram would have sent the invisible value, and
   231	   * `?text=visible&text=<invisible>` was REFUSED while Telegram would have sent the visible one. One
   232	   * bypass and one destroyed message from a single line. `URLSearchParams.get` returns the first.
   233	   */
   234	  /**
   235	   * STATED OPEN — the body's encoding is inferred from its JavaScript WRAPPER, never from `Content-Type`.
   236	   *
   237	   * Everything below branches on what kind of JS value the body is — a string, `URLSearchParams`,
   238	   * `FormData`, a stream. Telegram decides from the request's media type. A caller sending a JSON
   239	   * string under a form content type, or the reverse, is read one way here and another way there, and
   240	   * the field this door checks is then not the field Telegram reads.
   241	   *
