docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:10:  - "P19 No Unbounded Loops (rate-floored, capped, in-flight-aware tmp sweep)"
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:54:**Why spawn, not execFile:** `execFile`'s `maxBuffer` overflow does not truncate — it kills the child and fails the call. The `--json` event stream is strictly larger than today's plain output, so a buffered approach makes long calls — the cartographer-sweep class this directive targets — *more* likely to fail than today. Streaming bounds memory instead:
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:79:**Audit-volume consequence (engaged, not discovered at CI):** routing per-call `finally` deletions through `SafeFsExecutor` makes `.instar/audit/destructive-ops.jsonl` a hot-path log (~1,550 appends/day on a codex-routed agent) with — today — no rotation anywhere, growing without bound AND drowning the rare wipe-class entries the audit exists to surface. The same PR therefore adds a **size-capped rotation to `appendAuditEntry`** (rotate at 16 MB, keep one predecessor) — benefiting every SafeFs/SafeGit caller, not just this one. **Rotation posture pinned:** rename-based atomic rotation (`current → .1`) with a **re-stat immediately before the rename, skipping if below cap** — without it, two writer processes racing the threshold can double-rotate and the second rename clobbers the FRESHEST just-rotated 16 MB segment (the loss lands on the newest audit history, the worst direction for an audit log; the re-stat shrinks that window to effectively zero, and the live log is never at risk either way). The **first entry of each fresh segment is a rotation-marker entry** (timestamp, aged-out entry count, predecessor name), so "the audit log shrank" stays auditable — legitimate rotation must never be camouflage for audit-log loss (Observation Needs Structure). Fixture covers rotation + marker + the below-cap-skip. L12's guarantee (every destructive op audited) is unweakened; only retention is bounded.
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:86:2. **Thread-event shape (only when no protocol event appeared):** `{"type":"turn.completed","usage":{...}}` is per-turn — **summed** across all `turn.completed` events.
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:98:`CircuitBreakingIntelligenceProvider.evaluate`'s error path forwards captured usage into the metric row: `tokensIn`, `tokensOut`, AND `tokensCached` (the variable is in scope at `:204-210`). Unit fixture: usage-event-then-extraction-failure → the `'error'` row carries real tokens.
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:102:- The drift condition is **zero usage RECORDED** (post-validation, post-reconciliation) from a successful `--json` call — not "zero events parsed," which a reconciliation-rot would never trip, and **including the empty-stream case**: exit 0 with an empty event stream under `--json` is itself anomalous (a future codex that succeeds while emitting nothing reproduces exact token-blindness), so it counts as drift with `reason: empty-stream` rather than falling between the tripwire and the canary. Emission: DegradationReporter **legacy `.report()`** (the only path with a live consumer today; `reportStructured` has no consumer until the Remediator ships) with the **fixed feature constant `codex-usage-parse-drift`** (the feature string is the Telegram dedup key — a fixed constant prevents the P17 unique-source dodge), `reason` payload distinguishing `no-events` / `empty-stream` / `reconciliation-failed` / `shape-divergence` / `oversized-lines-discarded`. **Emission is gated to once per process lifetime** (module-level flag) — DegradationReporter's legacy path files an external feedback report per event with no feedback-side cooldown, so an hourly emission would spam the maintainer webhook ~24×/day/machine forever (P17). Once-per-boot is sufficient: the durable, always-current surface is `usageCoverage`, not the event. (`scripts/lint-degradation-emit-sites.js` auto-discovers `.report()` sites by scanning `src/` — no manual catalogue exists to edit; the new site is picked up automatically.)
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:103:- `totals` in `/metrics/features` gains `usageCoverage`: rows keyed per framework, with NULL-framework rows bucketed `"unknown"` (mirroring `byFeatureModel`'s NULL-model rendering). **Denominator = successful llm rows only (`fired + noop`)** — error rows legitimately lack usage (claude parses usage only on success; codex timeouts can kill the child pre-flush), and including them would normalize coverage below 1.0 and train operators to ignore the tripwire. Error rows are reported alongside as `errorRows` / `errorRowsWithUsage` (which also surfaces the error-path recording directly). **Computability pin:** usage-presence is a NULL test, not a SUM (a recorded `0` must count as reported; one large row must not mask N null rows) — so the single `GROUP BY feature, model, framework` query carries the presence counts itself: `successRowsWithUsage = COUNT(CASE WHEN outcome IN ('fired','noop') THEN tokens_in END)` and `errorRowsWithUsage = COUNT(CASE WHEN outcome='error' THEN tokens_in END)` (SQLite `COUNT(expr)` counts non-NULL). `usageCoverage` is then derived in JS from the same partition — no second window scan. Exemption is keyed **per provider implementation, not per framework**: rows with `model: 'interactive-pool'` are excluded from the claude-code denominator (that provider NEVER invokes `onUsage` by documented contract — under `subscriptionPath.mode: auto|force`, pool-routed calls would otherwise indistinguishably depress claude coverage); `gemini-cli` rows are `exempt: true`; **pi-cli is NOT exempt** — `PiCliIntelligenceProvider` already invokes `onUsage`, and exempting it would mask a future pi parse-rot. Expected coverage for non-exempt providers is ~1.0 against the success-only denominator.
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:104:- **Live canary (L5):** an integration-tier test runs ONE real `codex exec --json` call asserting usage is recorded. **Skip-vs-fail boundary:** named-skip when the binary is absent OR the failure is environmental (auth error, rate-limit signature, network error, non-zero exit with empty stream). **FAIL on the discriminating cases: successful exit + zero recorded usage — whether the stream was non-empty (parse rot) or empty (emission rot).** A canary that reddens on weather gets skip-listed — which is the suppression path that re-opens the rot.
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:110:- `CodexCliIntelligenceProviderOptions` gains `resolveExecJson?: () => boolean`. `evaluate()` reads it **per call** (the IntelligenceRouter caches built providers; a per-call closure read survives that caching). **Closure body pinned** — the two naive implementations are both wrong: a closure over the boot-time in-memory config object never sees a disk flip (no config hot-reload exists), and a per-call `loadConfig()` drags a synchronous SecretStore keychain `execFileSync` (~50–150 ms) onto every judgment call. The closure does a **TTL-cached (30 s) raw `readFileSync` + `JSON.parse` of `.instar/config.json` reading ONLY `intelligence.codexExecJson`** — never `loadConfig()`, never the secrets merge. **Resolution order:** the config key wins when the file parses AND the key is present; otherwise — including parse/read failure — fall through to `process.env.INSTAR_CODEX_EXEC_JSON !== '0'` (itself default-on). This keeps BOTH levers honest on every path (the error text names both; an env lever that closure-threaded paths ignored would be a broken rollback contract), and un-strands the realistic incident case: an operator hand-editing config under pressure who leaves a JSON slip gets a once-per-process parse-failure warning and a still-working env lever, not a silently-ignored kill-switch. Fixture: corrupt config + env=0 → plain mode. A config flip applies within 30 s, no restart.
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:123:- New aggregation `byFeatureModel(opts)` — `GROUP BY feature, model, framework`: `{ feature, model, framework, calls, realCalls, tokensIn, tokensOut, tokensCached, fired, noop, errors, shed, successRowsWithUsage, errorRowsWithUsage }[]`. NULL model renders `"unknown"`. **No latency percentiles on this dimension** (the percentile pass is the one full-row-materializing query and stays single-keyed; a future per-model percentile must reuse one composite-key query, never a second full-window load). **`summary()` calls `byFeatureModel` ONCE and partitions rows in JS** — per-feature slicing queries are forbidden (N× full-window scans on synchronous better-sqlite3). **Scan-neutral:** the per-feature `frameworks[]`/`models[]` arrays that `byFeature` currently computes via a separate DISTINCT window scan are derivable from this partition (its group keys ARE feature×framework×model) — `summary()` sources them from the partition and drops the now-subsumed DISTINCT scan, so the slice lands at net-zero window scans instead of +1. No new DDL beyond the one ADDED_COLUMNS entry; identical failure envelope to `byFeature` on a DB whose ALTER was swallowed.
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:131:**The baseline is driven to zero in this PR.** The verified unattributed funnel callsites — `src/threadline/WarrantsReplyGate.ts:295`, `src/threadline/PipeSessionSpawner.ts:158` + `:228`, `src/security/LLMSanitizer.ts:107`, `src/providers/uxConfirm/OverrideDetector.ts:128`, `src/providers/uxConfirm/TaskClassifier.ts:123`, `src/providers/adapters/anthropic-interactive-pool/index.ts:200` (canary completion judge), `src/messaging/slack/SlackAdapter.ts:827` — each gain `attribution: { component: '<Name>' }`. (CoherenceGate/SendGateway/AutoDispatcher were checked and are NOT violators — their LLM calls route through already-attributed components.) This is not scope creep; it is the directive itself — those calls are exactly the unaudited spend the operator named. **`LLMSanitizer` note:** its options come from a constructor *property assignment* (`this.defaultOptions`), which the lint's same-file-declaration rule does not cover — the fix there is the sanctioned inline shape at the callsite (`{...this.defaultOptions, attribution: {…}}`), not a constructor edit that would leave the lint red. **Every newly-attributed component is REGISTERED in `COMPONENT_CATEGORY` (`src/core/componentCategories.ts`) in the same PR** — an unregistered name resolves to `'other'` → the default framework, silently excluding it from per-component framework routing and under-reporting `GET /intelligence/routing` coverage. A wiring test asserts every literal `attribution.component` in `src/` resolves to a registered (non-`'other'`) category or a documented exclusion — and **that exclusion list joins the same ratchet test** (which therefore pins THREE lists: violations allowlist, FUNNEL_FILES, wiring-test exclusions; additions to any fail CI), because an unpinned exclusion surface would be the same dodge channel one layer over.
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:134:- Scans `src/` for funnel callsites: `.evaluate(` on intelligence providers/router handles (receiver matches `/intelligence|provider|llm/i` or threads `IntelligenceOptions`). Out of scope by construction: `src/providers/parity/scenarios/` and `_smoketest`/`_stresstest` files call one-shot adapters directly (they bypass the funnel and record no ledger rows); a lint self-test asserts the heuristic does not flag them, since the empty allowlist would otherwise redden CI on non-funnel calls.
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:138:- Sanctioned fix for the cross-file-spread shape (`evaluate(p, {...sharedOpts})`) is **inlining `attribution:` at the callsite** — never allowlisting. Stated in the lint header, with the known accepted limitations (conditional attribution and helper-wrapper indirection pass lexically; runtime backstop below).
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:139:- Wired into the `package.json` lint chain (pre-commit runs it via `npm run lint` — no direct husky edit), with `lint:llm-attribution` / `:staged` entries.
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:145:Family: **Shipping**. Heading: `### Token-Audit Completeness — An Unmetered LLM Call Is an Unaccountable One`. Structured for `StandardsRegistryParser` (verified: `###` under a family H2, non-empty `**Rule.**`):
docs/specs/token-audit-completeness.md:155:- `src/scaffold/templates.ts` → the "Per-Feature LLM Metrics" CLAUDE.md section gains the per-model breakdown, `usageCoverage`, and unlabeled-share mention. `migrateClaudeMd()` is **append-only by house policy** — this ships as an appended addendum block keyed on a NEW content-sniff marker — **the literal `unlabeledCallShare`** (a REAL field name that appears in the addendum text; the earlier `unlabeledShare` literal is a substring of NEITHER actual field, so an addendum written with the real names would never self-match and the migration would re-append on every update run, violating idempotency). The **shadow-mirror list entries** (`migrateFrameworkShadowCapabilities` uses markers as `indexOf` slice ANCHORS and section BOUNDARIES, not mere presence sniffs — so a marker must be line-leading): the new addendum's heading, plus — for the base section, which the shadow mirror never carried — **two tail-truncated, line-leading literals**: `**Per-Feature LLM Metrics` (templates' bold-block variant) and `### Per-Feature LLM Metrics` (covers both H3 variants: migrateClaudeMd's current form and the older deployed form). Tail-truncation is the list's sanctioned multi-variant trick (cf. the MTP marker); a HEAD-truncated bare phrase would anchor mid-heading (dropping the `###`/`**` prefix in the mirrored copy) and — worse — match the phrase inside OTHER sections' body prose, truncating their slices at it (the new addendum's own body names the phrase, so it would be the first victim). Each CLAUDE.md contains exactly one variant, so exactly one literal fires; the other no-ops. The updated `templates.ts` base section also emits the `unlabeledCallShare` literal so freshly-initialized agents self-match the addendum sniff and are never double-patched (house convention).
docs/review/track2-memory-pipeline.md:4:Instar implements a three-layer memory system: JSONL append-only logs (source of truth), SQLite with FTS5 full-text search (derived query layer), and a WorkingMemoryAssembler that token-budgets context from semantic knowledge, episodic digests, and relationships. Rolling summaries use Haiku ("fast" model) triggered every 20 new messages. MEMORY.md is agent-authored, not Instar-managed.
