docs/specs/time-claim-subject-binding.eli16.md:3:TIME_CLAIM exists to stop an agent from guessing how long its active autonomous session has been running. Before a message is delivered, it recognizes a statement such as “seven hours elapsed” or “two hours remaining” and compares that number with the real session clock. On July 23, that useful check interrupted two correct Echo messages for the wrong reason. One message described the length of an offline test window; another described detection latency. Neither said how long the session had been running, but both looked enough like duration phrases to the old extractor that it compared them with the session clock. Echo had to use the audited advisory override twice.
docs/specs/time-claim-subject-binding.eli16.md:5:The fix adds the missing semantic boundary: a quantity and unit are not enough; the verifier must also bind the thing being measured. An anchored duration remains a session-clock candidate when the text explicitly names the run, session, clock, or time box. Existing unqualified shapes such as “seven hours elapsed” also remain candidates because the caller supplies an active session clock and that behavior is the original safety contract. However, when the local clause names a competing duration subject—such as a test window, latency, ETA, timeout, CI queue, outage, or recovery—the candidate is dropped instead of being compared with the session.
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:25:A central **Remediator** that gives the five healers a shared brain:
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:27:- Shared logbook (every attempt across every healer goes here).
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:28:- Shared lock (one heal of the same type at a time).
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:29:- Shared cooldown (stop after 3 attempts in 4 hours; tell the user).
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:30:- Shared dry-run mode (new healers run observe-only for a week before they're allowed to act).
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:31:- Shared silence-vs-alert decision (silence earned by verified success; alerts for novel failures and verification failures).
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:59:- Healers can't both try to fix the same problem at once: they acquire a shared HMAC-signed lockfile first. Lockfile content is signed; replays are caught via heartbeat sequence numbers.
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:64:- "Verify failed" requires a probe-signed structured failure payload — anything else (timeout, missing reply, malformed payload) is "inconclusive" and doesn't count toward quarantine. A compromised probe can't DoS healing just by being noisy.
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:81:The cross-model panel pushed back on bundling everything into Phase 1. Now tiered:
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:91:Cross-model panel surfaced that the original design hard-required OS keychain — which breaks Docker, headless CI, airgapped, and Windows. Fix: the key vault supports four backends in priority order:
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:93:1. OS keychain (macOS Keychain or Linux libsecret) — preferred.
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:94:2. Hardware enclave (TPM 2.0, Secure Enclave bridge) — preferred when present.
docs/specs/SELF-HEALING-REMEDIATOR-V2-SPEC.eli16.md:113:2. **Is the tiered rollout (Minimum Safe Core → Hardening → Fleet Intelligence) the right phasing?** Or do you want everything in one Phase 1?
