docs/positioning-vs-openclaw.md:58:- Auth-secured HTTP API for session/job/relationship management
docs/positioning-vs-openclaw.md:87:**OpenClaw** runs a WebSocket gateway with `sessions_spawn` for multi-session support and model-tiered sub-agents. Multiple agents are supported through routing rules with deterministic priority hierarchy.
docs/positioning-vs-openclaw.md:95:**OpenClaw's SOUL.md** is elegant. On first run, the agent and user have a bootstrap conversation: "Who am I? Who are you?" The result is a self-authored identity file the agent can modify over time. It's personal and charming.
docs/positioning-vs-openclaw.md:149:| **Multi-Agent Routing** | Deterministic priority hierarchy. Per-agent workspace, sandbox, tool policy, model config. Route different contacts to different "brains." | Well-engineered. |
docs/positioning-vs-openclaw.md:152:| **Browser Automation** | CDP + Playwright. AI-friendly numbered element refs. Tri-target (local, extension, remote). Sandbox-aware with VNC. | Well-designed. |
docs/positioning-vs-openclaw.md:161:| **Voice Wake** | Wake word detection on paired devices. | Documented at `/nodes/voicewake`. Supported on macOS, iOS, Android. |
docs/positioning-vs-openclaw.md:209:LLM-powered stall detection and recovery. Session health monitoring across all active sessions. Promise tracking (detects "working on it" and follows up if no response). Loud degradation reporting with zero silent fallbacks. The agent monitors itself and recovers from failures autonomously.
docs/positioning-vs-openclaw.md:258:| **Self-healing** | Loop detection | LLM-powered stall triage, session monitoring, promise tracking |
docs/positioning-vs-openclaw.md:319:- Self-healing (LLM-powered stall recovery, session monitoring, promise tracking -- not just loop detection)
