docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass25-verdict.md:9:Pass 25 recorded load-bearing **1** — a new series floor — and credited BOTH clauses of the acceptance
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass25-verdict.md:12:window: *"The decline measured the exhaustion of the repair-chase, not the exhaustion of defects."*
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass25-verdict.md:22:**Grading rule, declared up front (the passes 17–24 rule, kept verbatim for comparability):** a finding is load-bearing if it either **(i)** leaves a guard unable to refuse an input it claims to refuse, or **(ii)** leaves a committed artifact asserting something false about the machinery. I apply clause (ii) the way pass 24 applied it — as *"checkably false in a committed artifact"* — and name the boundary where the strict reading changes the count.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass25-verdict.md:40:**3. SEVERITY: nit — "Proven on a real committed line, not a fixture" heads evidence that demonstrates the DEFECT under the two prior rules and never the repair; and at HEAD the sentence beneath it reads backwards.** The claim is true; the evidence offered does not establish it. I ran it: the HEAD rule refuses and the pass-23 rule reports clean, in both directions.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass25-verdict.md:44:**5. SEVERITY: nit — a residual the repair creates is not among the three the header declares.** A retired wording quoted WITH its leading emphasis marker becomes unmatchable at a wrap. Zero live instances.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass25-verdict.md:52:All eight claimed repairs (a)–(h) verified CLEAN, with (d) PARTIAL as a class: the named misquotation is byte-exact against its archive once the elision is restored, but a full sweep found two other truncations. The marker rule verified in both directions on the real committed boundary and on two wrap shapes pass 24 did not test. The three emitted-text corruptions verified from the OUTPUT, not the source. The self-count now derives from the same array it iterates, so it cannot diverge again. The archive limb fired twice under my hand.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass25-verdict.md:68:**Both clauses met.** Magnitude declining for the seventh consecutive pass, without qualification, on a new floor. Remainder converted to dated named work: every undated finding I have is a nit or a bookkeeping error that misleads no machinery, which the criterion explicitly disclaims as blocking; everything else is on the declared, dated, operator-ruled list, and I reproduced the largest of them rather than taking it on trust.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass25-verdict.md:70:I considered rejecting seriously, because nineteen would be an easy number to write. I decline it on three grounds: finding 1 is not a capability defect; it errs by understatement; and the criterion names bookkeeping errors that mislead no machinery as explicitly non-blocking.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:14:contested-then-cleared: 0
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:40:The system IS self-healing correctly. The defect is narration: it buzzes at decision priority on states it is mid-way through healing, speaks twice per episode, and its loud-escalation backstops are anchored on clocks that reset under the exact churn they measure.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:44:1. **Alert = action needed or persistent.** A condition the system predictably heals is not operator-actionable; only a STALLED heal is. This is **Self-Heal Before Notify** for the version dimension: the heal is the auto-updater; observable version advance is `selfHealAttempted`; stall past the ceiling is `selfHealExhausted`; the ceiling is the `max-notification-latency` backstop — and per that standard, the operator-facing BUZZ sits downstream of heal exhaustion, never on raw detection.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:47:4. **Never quieter than the operator can still see somewhere deliverable.** Every predicate failure falls back to LOUDER; every demotion has a live deliverability check or a documented accepted consequence.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:53:*Design alternative considered and rejected:* an append-only event log with reducers (event-sourced episodes/eligibility/digest) would also solve restart/handoff correctness, but it replaces the episode manager's existing durable-state model wholesale — a foundation rewrite for a narration change, contradicting principle 3 (signal-only) and multiplying blast radius. The anchors extend the existing model additively instead.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:60:- **Clock model (suspension is computable):** clocks are the machine-local wall clock at advert observation. The key's `activeSkewMs` accumulates `min(now − anchorLastEvaluatedAtMs, K × tickMs)` (K small, e.g. 4), **clamped to `[0, K × tickMs]`** (a wall-clock regression can never decrement), and **credits ONLY reconciles in which the key is observed divergent AND every skew participant is present in the compared set** — a suspended anchor (participant departed) freezes BOTH the clear and the accumulator, so a ≥3-pool laggard offline for hours cannot tick its own ceiling while its heal is unobservable. Gaps longer than K ticks — offline dips, <2-online windows, sentinel restarts, warmup — contribute at most K ticks, retroactively treating the gap as suspended. Grace and the stall ceiling compare against `activeSkewMs`, never wall-clock-since-onset. Advances can NOT reset onset or the accumulator.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:61:- **Participant-aware clear (never vacuous):** convergence-clear requires EVERY machine that participated in the key's skew to be PRESENT in the compared set AND agreeing, sustained for `resolveTicks`; a skew participant leaving the compared set (offline / stale / unverifiable) suspends the anchor regardless of how many other members remain, and a compared set of <2 can never clear an anchor. (This is strictly stronger than the <2-online rule and mirrors the episode manager's participant-departure suspension — closing the ≥3-pool "blinking laggard resets the ceiling" hole.)
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:63:- **Progress definition (version dimension):** an "advance" counts only when the lagging machine's version moves strictly toward the pool's maximum observed version (numeric `major.minor.patch` triplet comparison; prerelease/build metadata ignored for ordering; unparseable ⇒ never an advance). The pool max is recomputed each tick from current adverts — a leader rollback shrinks the gap without laggard action (safe: smaller skew or convergence); a laggard regression is not an advance.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:65:- **Lifecycle:** EVERY per-key structure (anchor, progress state, flap history, latches) is dropped only after SUSTAINED absence — the key absent from the compared set for ≥24 h (covering the retire-return cycles that update waves and registry rebuilds cause; a transiently-departed key's latches survive their remaining window, so churn correlated with the tracked wave cannot re-arm a second `:stalled` HIGH inside the latch window). M6-suppressed flag rows SUSPEND their anchor, never retire it; the raw rows feeding anchors are the POST-suppression set. Bounds: keyspace ≤ the compared dimensions + manifest intersection (≤64 by the existing clamp); flap arrays clamped at 64 events (a fast-flapping flag can cycle in ~2.5 min — the arithmetic bound is the clamp, not the grace math). Per-machine participant/progress tracking (`{ lastSeenVersion, lastAdvanceAtMs }` + the persisted per-key PARTICIPANT SET) is kept for every dimension — the participant-aware clear and the flap-event definition read the participant set on all dimensions, not just version.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:66:- **Every-machine computation:** all machines run reconcile today (speaks() gates only effects), so every machine accumulates its own durable copy of the anchors from the same shared adverts — anchors survive raiser handoff (the new raiser has its own accumulated copy), restarts (durable file), and dips (the accumulator's bounded-gap rule). Residual honesty: a machine that never observed the skew's start (fresh join, corrupt-rebaseline) carries a LATER onset; if elected raiser, escalation stretches by that offset — bounded drift, direction unchanged. **The correctness contract, named:** this is *at-least-once, bounded-duplicate narration* (per-machine reconstruction from shared adverts) — never a single coherent global episode; every duplicate bound is disclosed (≤2× per handoff window per surface). Stronger semantics would require a shared event stream — rejected above as a foundation rewrite for a narration change.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:86:- **Derived-item lifecycle (complete):** the episode state durably tracks `derivedItemIds`. `closeEpisode` resolves the base AND every derived item. An episode that escalated closes with a NOTIFYING resolve note whose copy adapts to the close reason ("healed — the earlier stalled alert is withdrawn" / "that machine left the pool — the earlier alert no longer applies"): the operator was loudly asked to consider a decision, so the stand-down notifies (decision-withdrawal is action-relevant). **Note authorship across raiser handoff (item-holder voice):** narration state is per-machine and the raiser role migrates, so the withdrawal note is authored by the machine CLOSING ITS OWN escalated item — regardless of whether it currently speaks. That keeps one voice per ITEM (never zero notes when the old raiser holds the escalated item and the role moved), bounded by the already-accepted ≤2×-per-handoff residual when both machines escalated their own copies. Calm-only episodes close SILENT. Reopens append to the BASE item and never resurrect derived items. Unit obligation: "stall → heal → base AND derived items both DONE; exactly one notifying close note PER ITEM-HOLDING MACHINE (≤2 across a handoff window)."
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:89:- **Operator-interacted bit:** a durable per-episode `operatorInteracted` boolean set ONLY by authenticated operator actions — fix approval and explicit ack — with the setter carrying the same `verifiedOperator` evidence shape as `approveFix` (the ack path gains that parameter — named build obligation; never derived from `pendingFix`, which is auto-created at raise and cleared on every fix path). Rides the `resolve` effect. Interacted episodes close notifying. Reopen does not carry it. An ack racing the close loses toward silent (documented; the raise/escalation already informed).
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:94:- **Typed contract:** the prober's `escalate()` and `raiseAttention` dep gain `class: 'informational' | 'actionable'` (slow-alive → informational; exhaustion → actionable; UNDECLARED → actionable, fail-loud). Declared at the source that authored the text; no string parsing anywhere.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:95:- **Delivery-true demotion predicate:** an informational escalation is demoted to the audit log + digest ONLY when, on the raising machine, ALL hold LIVE at demotion time: `monitoring.ropeHealth` enabled, `digestTopicId` set, AND the digest job is actually runnable IN THIS PROCESS — defined as a live LOCAL handle check: a `JobScheduler` instance is running in this process AND the `rope-health-digest` job is loaded and enabled (the lease read is at most a fast-path pre-check, never the conjunct: the scheduler is constructed only at boot when awake, so a standby PROMOTED to lease-holder at runtime holds the lease with NO scheduler until restart — the lease proxy would pass exactly where nothing delivers). Any miss ⇒ fall back to today's hub attention item. Config presence is NOT delivery capability; the non-lease-holder and the promoted-standby both take the fallback arm (the promoted-standby case joins wiring test #4).
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:105:- Config placement: machine-coherence keys under `monitoring.machineCoherence` (boot-read, like siblings); the rope filter key under `monitoring.ropeHealth` (boot-read; noted: the prober's OTHER knobs live under `multiMachine.meshTransport`); the demotion predicate's lease check is a live runtime read by design.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:110:- **Fail-loud escalation invariant:** with transients at zero buzz, the derived raises are the guard's only buzz path — so an anchor whose ceiling/flap threshold fired WITHOUT a corresponding derived item id recorded within one tick increments `escalationRaiseFailed`, writes a jsonl row, and raises ONE plain attention item ("my escalation path failed — check the guard") — the calm system may never fail silent about its own loud arm.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:127:| Patch-skew confirmation timing (M-P1) | `invariant` | Deterministic over durable anchors + shared adverts (gap-narrowing within window; ceiling on `activeSkewMs`); conservative default (no-advance ⇒ confirm ⇒ louder); ceiling unresettable by construction; flap brake + clamps bound repetition. |
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:130:| Rope routing (M-P3) | `invariant` | Source-declared typed class AND a live deliverability conjunction; undeclared ⇒ actionable ⇒ hub (fail-loud). |
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:138:- **Orphan self-closeout (the handoff residual, closed):** today a handoff mid-episode leaves the old raiser's items open forever (resolve effects are speaks()-gated) — a defect this spec's derived items would have enlarged. Fixed structurally: notifying resolve NOTES follow the item-holder-voice rule (M-P2), but item STATUS resolution is decoupled from speaks() — the decoupling is the machine's own `closeEpisode` status-resolution arm executed regardless of speaks(), for EVERY close reason (restored, expired-peer-gone, manifest-changed), EPISODE-scoped (never per-anchor-key: an episode spanning several keys resolves only when the manager's own all-rows-cleared close fires), silently transitioning that machine's OWN store's open items (base + derived) to DONE. No machine's items depend on it being the current speaker; no manual cleanup exists. Residual honestly bounded: up to 2× the per-day cap across a handoff window (two machines each opened items before convergence) — both copies self-close on close. Unit obligation: a non-speaking machine on an `expired-peer-gone` close transitions its own items DONE with no note.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:149:5. **Progress signal source:** shared coherence adverts only; gap-narrowing toward the recomputed pool max; numeric-triplet ordering, prerelease/build ignored, regression ≠ advance.
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:151:7. **Calm-raise notification mode (revised round 2):** SILENT by default — the Near-Silent Notifications standard mandates it ("a monitor reporting a non-actionable internal verdict is default-silent"); prerequisite honored in the same increment: the participant-aware unresettable ceiling (M-P0) is what makes zero-buzz safe. `calmRaiseNotify: true` is the rollback lever; the wave backstop covers the cross-key pattern; the derived escalation raises get a wiring test proving they reach `createAttentionItem` NON-silent (with transients at zero buzz they are the guard's only buzz-bearing path — safety-critical, not cosmetic).
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:163:- Episode-lifecycle redesign beyond the named touchpoints; duplicate-reconciliation; fix-it/leave-it consent flow. (The handoff-orphan cleanup is IN scope — the self-closeout rule in the Multi-machine posture section — so the orphan cleanup ships complete in this build.)
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:167:- **Unit:** M-P0 anchor lifecycle (onset survives identity churn/advances; participant-aware clear — 3-machine blinking laggard does not clear; singleton set never clears; accumulator bounded-gap arithmetic incl. warmup/restart gaps; ceiling on `activeSkewMs` with "N successive advances still confirm"); gap-narrowing (regression/lateral/unparseable never advance; leader rollback shrinks gap safely); flap event = confirm + genuine convergence (re-mints/bare re-confirms never count); per-key 24 h derived-raise latches (episode churn cannot multiply `:stalled`); priority/copy/silent mapping incl. enum clamping; resolve bounding + latched-flapping-to-jsonl; `operatorInteracted` (auto-pendingFix never sets; evidence-carrying ack sets; reopen clears; race loses silent); escalated-close notifying vs calm-close silent; wave backstop threshold + 24 h dedupe; rope typed-class routing (informational demotes only under the full live conjunction; undeclared ⇒ actionable; both-class 24 h sink dedupe + count appends).
docs/specs/calm-transient-episode-alerting.md:171:- **Semantic-boundary coverage:** both sides of every decision boundary with realistic inputs — calm vs loud class selection (patch vs major-minor vs stall vs flap), silent vs notifying mode (interacted vs untouched vs escalated close), demote vs fallback (each conjunct of the deliverability predicate individually failing), extend vs confirm (advance inside vs outside the window).
