docs/specs/reports/window12-laptop/axis-enforcement.out:6:2. **Operator-Surface Quality** — claims `scripts/instar-dev-precommit.js` “blocks a commit touching an operator surface unless the review answers the quality question in writing” — this detects a written declaration, not whether the surface is usable, plain-language, mobile-safe, or visually sound. The surface is plausible and shared by other articles, but the article explicitly uses this receipt to classify itself as enforced.
docs/specs/reports/window12-laptop/axis-enforcement.out:7:3. **Side-Effects Review Gate** — says “The review is a structural gate” enforced by a staged side-effects artifact — the gate certifies only artifact presence and minimum length, not review of the five required dimensions, reviewer independence, or a substantive verdict. The article eventually admits this is “willpower with a receipt,” but only after the strong enforcement claim.
docs/specs/reports/window12-laptop/axis-enforcement.out:8:4. **Dashboard UX Standard** — claims “eleven objective, CI-enforced floors” — its enforcement section names tests only for F1, F2, F9, F10, and F11; F3–F8 are explicitly follow-up work. Those tests plausibly detect real covered UI breaches, but six of the eleven advertised floors do not exist yet; the correction is buried at the end of a long Applied-through paragraph.
docs/specs/reports/window12-laptop/axis-enforcement.out:9:5. **Agent Proposes, Operator Approves** — claims a precommit assertion “blocks a commit … unless answered in writing,” plus one server-authored reference implementation — the generic gate detects an attestation, not whether every approval surface is pre-filled or display-integrity-safe. The named infrastructure is plausible, but only the first implementation has behavioral structure.
docs/specs/reports/window12-laptop/axis-enforcement.out:14:10. **Canonical Pipeline Operational Completeness — Accepted Intake Must Drain** — claims a manifest, lint, and named runtime positive control enforce operational completeness — the lint detects declared stages, citations, metadata, and collected test entries; it explicitly cannot certify actual liveness, cadence, idempotency, or progress, and an undeclared intake can escape the closed manifest. Plausible machinery, but mostly structural declaration rather than proof that intake drains.
docs/specs/reports/window12-laptop/axis-enforcement.out:19:15. **One Failure Teaches Every Guard** — claims gap-record and fingerprint lints make each failure sweep every guard — they enforce freshness, partitioning, and reasons over voluntarily authored records. They cannot detect an unrecorded failure, judge sweep quality, or require a match to be remediated. The article accurately calls this “freshness bookkeeping,” but only deep inside extensive enforcement prose.
docs/specs/reports/window12-laptop/axis-enforcement.out:20:16. **Maturation Path — Test Agent → Development Agent → Fleet** — claims `FeatureMaturationPlanGate` refuses convergence without a complete plan — that detects the declaration required by clause (a), not graduation-evidence quality, ship-time registration, routing, or whether the ladder is ever walked. Three of four clauses remain unenforced; the named gate is plausible but certifies paperwork.
docs/specs/reports/window12-laptop/axis-enforcement.out:22:18. **Mobile-Complete Operator Actions** — claims one Mandates UI, migrated guidance, and an operator-surface review question — the first implementation may detect its own behavior, while the generic mechanism only asks for a declaration and does not enumerate operator-required actions. The article’s known missing approval-link generalization is visible, but it is not labeled documented-only despite the universal “every action” rule.
docs/specs/reports/window12-laptop/axis-enforcement.out:23:19. **Live-User-Channel Proof Before Done** — claims `LiveTestGate` refuses done/shipped without a verified artifact — this can detect a real missing artifact for a feature declared user-facing and configured in veto mode. It cannot detect an undeclared user-facing feature, and dry-run/warn modes do not block. The limitation is stated in Applied-through, but the article title and completion-gate language invite broader trust.
docs/specs/reports/window12-laptop/axis-enforcement.out:24:20. **A Decision That Can Block Must Live Where the Checks Can See It** — claims `lint-blocking-decisions-declared.mjs` covers the blocking population — it detects declarations, vanished files, ceiling growth, and changed bytes in files declared non-blocking. A changed file already declared blocking is silent, and completeness depends on the seeded census. It guards population bookkeeping, not continued correctness of blocking decisions.
docs/specs/reports/window12-laptop/axis-enforcement.out:27:23. **Deferral = Deletion** — claims a “FUNCTION check rather than an existence check” through `lint-deferral-referent-resolves.mjs` — the function actually tests whether an identifier occurs in selected executable/structured repository content. It cannot detect capture-now breaches, distinguish abandoned from completed work, or verify terminal follow-through. The article eventually documents this honestly, but only after an unusually strong claim and extensive corrective history.
